        "text": "Personal conviction should outweigh polling data when making political decisions.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Polls are unreliable indicators of future electoral success or public sentiment.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Running for office requires courage and independence rather than responsiveness to public opinion.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker has successfully won elections despite ignoring poll feedback.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      },
      {
        "text": "Public opinion measured by polls is often misinterpreted or misleading.",
        "confidence": 0.83
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Correction / Challenge"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 83368,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83368.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 97,
    "turn_text": "Well, no, here's the thing. We have to we have to earn the re-elect. That is without any question, the right place to be in a democracy, you got to earn the real act. And so our job, you are correct. We have a, we have historic accomplishments in terms of the economy, but we've done to move forward. It's science, technology and investment in the American workforce, growing the American workforce, rising wages, bringing down prices. Historic work has happened. No question. It is incumbent on us. to let people know who brung it to them, frankly, because here's the thing, Katie, when if you do want to look at polling, let's also look at the fact that it tells us that if you ask people how they feel about the fact that we finally cap the cost of insulin for seniors at $35 a month, hugely popular. If you ask people, how do you feel about the fact that seniors too long, too many had to determine whether they could fill their prescriptions or fill their refrigerator. And now we have allowed Medicare to negotiate drug prices, finally, which means we're capping the annual cost of prescriptions for seniors at $2,000. They're not going to pay more than $2,000 a year for their prescription drugs. Very, very popular. If you ask students with debt, the students of people who have graduated with debt, or even haven't graduated, who have debt from college or second years. And you just announced today additional debt relief for... We have now erased the debt of over three and a half million people. Very popular. If you look at what we are doing, that is right now, active projects in all 50 states that are about upgrading roads and bridges, repairing streets and airports. usually popular. And I, you know, if you drive down most streets in America, you'll somewhere or another see some construction site or a crane up that you can probably point to the Biden-Harris administration to know that that work is starting to happen because we finally got the infrastructure work done. Previous administration kept talking about infrastructure week. We actually made it happen. So you're right. We have a lot of accomplishments. And I think what the American people want most in their leaders is that we actually get things done. And we have done it. We haven't taken out a quick credit for it, frankly. And we got to do a better job of getting the word out about what we have accomplished and who did it.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_06",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The Biden-Harris administration has earned the right to be re-elected through its achievements.",
